---

<ServicePage
  title="Tree Trimming & Pruning in South Florida | Free Estimates"
  description="Free estimates for tree trimming, pruning and palm trimming in South Florida from independent local pros, plus what to avoid: topping and over-pruning."
  service={{
    name: "Tree Trimming and Pruning Estimates",
    serviceType: "Tree trimming and pruning estimate matching",
    description: "Free estimates for tree trimming, pruning and palm trimming from independent local tree care professionals in South Florida.",
  }}
  badge="TREE TRIMMING"
  h1="Tree Trimming & Pruning in South Florida"
  lead="Keep trees healthy, strong and clear of your home. Get a free estimate from an independent local professional."
  intro={[
    "Regular trimming is the cheapest way to avoid an expensive tree problem. In South Florida, fast growth and storm season mean branches reach roofs, wires and pools quickly, and dead limbs become a risk in the next storm.",
    "Send one request and we connect you with an independent tree care professional who gives you a free estimate. We are active in the Fort Lauderdale area and adding professionals elsewhere; see <a href=\"/areas/#coverage\">where we are active</a>. We don't do the work ourselves, so you compare the quote and agree the scope directly.",
  ]}
  coversTitle="What You Can Request"
  covers={[
    { name: "Tree trimming", note: "Shaping and thinning to keep branches off the roof, walls, wires and walkways, and to let light through." },
    { name: "Structural pruning", note: "Removing dead, diseased or weakly attached limbs and building a strong structure, ahead of hurricane season." },
    { name: "Palm trimming", note: "Removing dead fronds, seed pods and coconuts from royal, coconut and other palms near houses and pools." },
    { name: "Canopy lifting and clearance", note: "Raising the crown over driveways, roofs and sidewalks, and clearing branches that hang over a neighbor's property." },
    { name: "Storm cleanup", note: "Removing broken limbs and hangers after a storm. For anything on a structure, use the emergency form." },
    { name: "Tree assessment", note: "A health and risk check by an arborist when you are unsure whether a tree should be pruned or removed." },
  ]}
  detailTitle="What Good Trimming Looks Like"
  detail={`<p>Trimming done badly can harm or kill a tree. Look for these signs of a professional job:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No topping.</strong> Cutting the top off a tree, or cutting limbs back to stubs, weakens it. UF/IFAS research shows topped trees decay and break more easily.</li>
  <li><strong>No excessive thinning.</strong> Stripping the inside of the crown, sometimes called “hurricane cuts”, does not make a tree safer. A wind-resistant tree is pruned regularly to develop one strong trunk with well-spaced branches.</li>
  <li><strong>Pruning to a recognized standard</strong>, such as ANSI A300, with clean cuts at the right place on the branch.</li>
  <li><strong>A clear scope:</strong> which limbs, how much canopy, and whether cleanup and haul-away are included.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Before you book</h3>
<p>Ask for proof of license and insurance, and for the scope in writing. Some cities restrict how much of a tree can be removed, or protect certain trees, so check <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">the permit rules</a> if you are unsure. For limbs near power lines, call the utility instead of trimming yourself.</p>
<p>Also see <a href="/stump-grinding/">stump grinding</a>, <a href="/tree-removal/">tree removal</a> and our <a href="/tree-care-guides/hurricane-tree-preparation/">hurricane tree checklist</a>.</p>`}
  faqs={faqs}
  image="aerial-lift"
  introImage="canopy-climber"
  introTitle="Trimming That Keeps Trees Strong"
  extras={extras}
/>
